WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:09.190
Budapest, 16 June 1989. On the steps of an art hall on Heroes' Square lie six coffins.

00:00:09.270 --> 00:00:15.410
Four hold men executed after 1956, and one a man who died in prison.

00:00:15.490 --> 00:00:18.850
The sixth is empty, for all the dead.

00:00:18.930 --> 00:00:30.240
Some 200,000 to 250,000 people have come, 31 years to the day since Imre Nagy, prime minister in 1956, was executed.

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:38.340
How did Hungary get from a secret execution to this? This is what 1956 left behind.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:53.630
By December 1956 the revolution was crushed, and János Kádár, put in power by the Soviet army, governed Hungary.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:59.800
In December came the summary courts. A hidden weapon now meant at least ten years in prison.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:05.950
Anyone seen as a danger could be interned, held without trial for months.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:14.590
By the 1956 Institute's count, courts jailed more than 22,000 people, and even more were held without trial.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:19.280
In all, about one Hungarian in 200 lost their liberty.

00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:26.300
And 229 people were executed for their part in the revolution. Most had fought in it.

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:36.070
Even by the regime's own figures, at most 5 in 100 were "class enemies". Most were workers, peasants, clerks and students.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:44.150
Internment ended in 1961, and most prisoners went free only in 1963.

00:01:48.150 --> 00:01:55.260
Many did not wait. On 28 October, Austria had offered asylum to any Hungarian who asked.

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:00.230
After 4 November, people set out west in their tens of thousands.

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:07.450
Near the Austrian village of Andau, a narrow wooden bridge crossed the Einser canal, close to the border.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:13.400
From 6 November, about 70,000 people crossed it, by Austrian accounts.

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:20.020
On 21 November the bridge was blown up. People still crossed over its ruins.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:25.720
In December, as the reprisals began, a second, larger wave left.

00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:34.720
Hungary's statistics office counted 193,885 people who fled, most of them to Austria.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:40.210
Nearly two in three were men, and seven in ten were aged 15 to 39.

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:48.760
About 10,000 children under 15 left without their parents. And one in ten of Hungary's engineers left.

00:02:48.840 --> 00:03:00.110
The UN's refugee agency coordinated relief. The United States took some 44,000 people, and Canada some 39,000.

00:03:00.190 --> 00:03:12.410
Only 11,447 had come home by the end of May 1957. About 176,000 stayed abroad for good.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:23.400
Imre Nagy, prime minister during the revolution, had taken refuge in the Yugoslav embassy on 4 November.

00:03:23.480 --> 00:03:30.590
Pál Maléter, his defence minister, had been arrested the night before, at talks with the Soviet army.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:37.990
Kádár's government promised in writing that they could go home. On 22 November, they boarded a bus.

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:46.030
Soviet soldiers boarded too, armoured cars escorted the bus away, and the group was taken to Romania.

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:53.820
In early 1957, Kádár's party leaders, with Moscow's agreement, decided to put them on trial.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:04:02.840
The trial opened in February 1958, was halted at Moscow's request, and resumed in June behind closed doors.

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:10.510
The historian János Rainer concludes that the decisive, though not the only, responsibility was Kádár's.

00:04:10.590 --> 00:04:19.200
On 15 June 1958, the court sentenced Nagy, Maléter and the journalist Miklós Gimes to death.

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:27.720
Others got prison terms. Géza Losonczy had died in custody, and József Szilágyi had been executed in April.

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:34.240
Nagy did not ask for mercy. "I do not ask for clemency," he told the court.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:41.060
They were hanged at dawn on 16 June in a Budapest prison yard, and buried there without a mark.

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:44.050
Only then was the trial made public.

00:04:44.130 --> 00:04:57.530
In 1961 their coffins were moved in secret to plot 301 of a Budapest cemetery, among many executed after 1956, under false names.

00:05:01.330 --> 00:05:06.850
Kádár led the ruling party until 1988, and was twice prime minister.

00:05:06.930 --> 00:05:13.340
In 1961 he offered a bargain: "Whoever is not against us is with us."

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:21.690
In 1962, after a secret understanding with Washington, the UN dropped "the Hungarian question" from its agenda.

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:31.830
In March 1963 came an amnesty. Most of those jailed for 1956 went free; several hundred stayed in prison.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:37.520
From 1968 a reform brought some market rules into the planned economy.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:47.760
By official figures, real wages rose by half from 1960 to 1975, then stalled, and fell in the 1980s.

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:54.100
Some called it goulash communism: a softer dictatorship, but a dictatorship still.

00:05:54.180 --> 00:06:01.020
The other side of the bargain was silence. In school books, 1956 was a "counter-revolution".

00:06:01.100 --> 00:06:09.490
By the 1980s it had softened to "the regrettable events", but public commemoration was still forbidden.

00:06:12.690 --> 00:06:18.880
In May 1988, as the regime weakened, Kádár was replaced as party leader.

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:27.900
Former prisoners of 1956 founded a Committee for Historical Justice, and demanded the rehabilitation of the executed.

00:06:27.980 --> 00:06:36.850
On 16 June 1988, people gathered at the nameless graves. In the city, police beat demonstrators.

00:06:36.930 --> 00:06:46.420
Then, on 28 January 1989, a minister, Imre Pozsgay, called 1956 a "popular uprising".

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:53.620
That spring, the graves in plot 301 were opened, and Nagy and his companions identified.

00:06:53.700 --> 00:07:02.190
On 16 June 1989 came the funeral we began with, broadcast live on radio and television.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:06.880
Speakers called for the Soviet troops to leave, and for free elections.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:13.170
Speaking for the young, Viktor Orbán said that democracy and communism are incompatible.

00:07:13.250 --> 00:07:20.890
At the cemetery the names of the executed were read out, and the dead were buried again, with their names.

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:29.610
On 6 July the Supreme Court annulled the verdict of 1958. That same day, Kádár died.

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:43.720
On 23 October 1989, exactly 33 years after the revolution began, the People's Republic became the Republic of Hungary.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:51.120
The acting head of state, Mátyás Szűrös, proclaimed it from the balcony of Parliament, before tens of thousands.

00:07:51.200 --> 00:07:58.110
In 1990 came free elections, and a new law made 23 October a national day.

00:07:58.190 --> 00:08:04.100
On 19 June 1991, the last Soviet soldier left Hungary.

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:12.650
The students' first demand in 1956 had been that Soviet troops leave. It took 35 years.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:19.870
Today, on 23 October, flags with a hole fly again, as they did in 1956.

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:28.920
Plot 301 is now a national memorial park, and the bridge at Andau was rebuilt in 1996.

00:08:29.120 --> 00:08:41.080
1956 left many dead: fighters and bystanders, Hungarian and Soviet soldiers, victims of the secret police, and secret policemen lynched by crowds.

00:08:41.160 --> 00:08:47.450
It left some 200,000 lives begun again abroad, and three decades of silence at home.

00:08:47.530 --> 00:08:55.130
And it left names. For thirty years they were kept off the graves. Now they are back, and remembered.
